<%# One day of a universe's change history: every draft that closed on it, and every
    change each of those drafts remembered.

    **This partial is the shared half of the history surfaces.** The universe history
    page and the per-person one below **Collaborators** are the same reading at two
    scopes — one universe, and one author inside it — so the day grouping is here once
    rather than composed twice. A second copy is how two pages end up disagreeing about
    what a day contained, which is the same reason `drafts/_change` and
    `DraftsHelper#draft_history_sentence` are each shared.

    `changes` is the whole page's change collection, not this day's. It is passed down
    as the `changes` local of every `drafts/_change` row so that all of them share one
    `DraftChanges::Reader`: one query per record class for the page rather than one per
    row, which is finding 66 and the reason a universe-wide list is where that cost
    actually shows up. A day is a slice of the page, not a self-contained collection.

    **The date is `closed_at`, the day the run happened**, and the moment inside it is
    printed by the same helper that prints it on a draft's own page, so this page and
    that one cannot report two different times for one run. It is deliberately not a
    change's own `created_at`, which is when it was first remembered and can be days
    before it became live.

    `show_author` is false on the per-person page below **Collaborators**, whose own
    heading is the author's name: a row repeating it once per run is the same fact
    printed N times, and on that page it is the page's subject rather than a finding. %>
<% show_author = local_assigns.fetch(:show_author, true) %>
<% day = local_assigns.fetch(:day) %>
<% drafts = local_assigns.fetch(:drafts) %>
<% changes = local_assigns.fetch(:changes) %>
<section class="history-day" aria-labelledby="history-day-<%= day.iso8601 %>">
  <h2 class="history-day-heading h5" id="history-day-<%= day.iso8601 %>">
    <time datetime="<%= day.iso8601 %>"><%= l day, format: :long %></time>
  </h2>

  <% drafts.each do |draft| %>
    <%# **The run is a block, not a row of the list the changes sit in.** `drafts/_change`
        renders `list-group-item`s, and a list-group-item nested inside another one picks
        up the parent's padding and border and reads as a row inside a row. So the run's
        own heading is a plain container and the changes are the list, which is the
        shape `drafts/show` already uses for the same partial. %>
    <article class="history-draft content-surface">
      <header class="history-draft-header d-flex align-items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
        <%# The run's own heading links to the draft's page, which is where the full
            payload of each change is read: this list is the day, and a reader who wants
            the values behind a row follows the run. %>
        <%= link_to t("histories.draft_changes", count: draft.draft_changes.size),
          universe_draft_path(universe_slug: Current.universe.slug, id: draft),
          class: "entity-title" %>
        <span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-light border"><%= t("drafts.statuses.#{draft.status}") %></span>
        <%# Who closed it, where the page is not already about one person. %>
        <% if show_author %>
          <span class="small text-body-secondary"><%= draft.user.name %></span>
        <% end %>
      </header>

      <%# The same sentence the draft's own page prints, from the same helper: one run,
          one report, whether it is read here or there. %>
      <p class="history-draft-history entity-description draft-history">
        <%= draft_history_sentence(draft) %>
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="content-surface entity-list">
      <div class="list-group list-group-flush">
        <%= render partial: "drafts/change", collection: draft.draft_changes, as: :change,
          locals: { changes: changes } %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
